Chris Stratton <cs07024@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Given the whole mix of build-host binaries vs. target-host that aren't
> even remotely sorted out yet I don't have anything releasable yet, but
> stay posted.  It looks like maybe I'm going to get away with doing it
> "wrong", ie, using x86 linux binaries in util/ for the parts of make
> install that are supposed to be run on the target, apparently the
> formats are close enough.  Anyone know how to make it not require
> curses?   I'd already built ncurses to do rogue so I linked it in, but
> it would be nice to get rid of it.

The "standard" way to port NetHack to another platform with a new GUI
is to use the abstracted window methods to customize the look (like
the recently released iPhone port did).

But if you want to run it in a terminal emulator how could it *not*
need curses?

> In addition to cleaning up the build, I need to play with key bindings
> a bit and work out some means of telling the game to save when android
> kills off an idle session to reclaim its memory  (You don't really
> exit Android apps, idle ones just hang out in the background and can
> be killed off at any time, they are supposed to save on warning and
> then seamlessly resume return to the forefront)

NetHack already has a mechanism to save when receiving a signal.

That's for example used on public servers when the network connection
drops to let the game automatically save.

Grep for SIGHUP to see the places where the savehandler is registered.

